“COLONIZATION FAILED (AGAIN)”

Duration: 25 seconds
Style: Existential sci-fi horror, ancient biology vs fragile technology
Tone: Quiet dread, inevitability, cosmic indifference

ABSOLUTE RULES
NO establishing shots
NO static frames
NO heroic framing
Average shot length: 0.7–1.2s
Max shot length: 1.4s
Motion at all times (environmental, biological, camera micro-drift)

🎵 MUSIC (LOCKED)

A slow-burn, existential orchestral-electronic hybrid cue at ~48–56 BPM, built around extreme dynamic restraint and massive negative space, operating more like a geological process than a song.
Harmony & Tonality
Tonal center is ambiguous and drifting, hovering between low-register minor clusters rather than functional harmony
Sustained dissonant intervals (minor seconds, tritones, stacked fifths) that never fully resolve
Harmony behaves as pressure, not progression

Rhythm & Motion
Pulse is implied, not explicit — long-held tones create a sense of time stretching
No groove, no beat, no swing
Motion emerges through slow internal modulation, micro-swells, and evolving density
Instrumentation & Texture
Deep contrabass drones, low strings, and processed brass forming a subterranean bed
Granular electronic textures fused seamlessly with acoustic orchestra
Sub-bass frequencies felt physically rather than heard
High-register elements are sparse, brittle, and distant — like signals barely cutting through fog

Dynamics & Structure
Begins almost imperceptibly, as if the music has always been there
Gradual accumulation of mass: layers stack slowly, tension rising without release
Climax is not explosive — it is overwhelming by scale, like standing too close to something incomprehensibly large
Ends without catharsis; pressure remains unresolved
Production Aesthetic
Cold, minimal, monolithic

Reverb is vast but controlled — evokes cavernous, non-human spaces
No ornamentation, no melody for comfort
Sound design and orchestration are indistinguishable
Emotional / Conceptual Weight
Awe without triumph
Fear without panic
Intelligence without empathy
The feeling of encountering something ancient, immense, and utterly indifferent
Non-Negotiables (CWPP Locks)

❌ No rhythmic drums
❌ No melodic hooks
❌ No human warmth

✅ Emphasis on scale, pressure, and inevitability
✅ Music must feel discovered, not performed

Intent:
Sub-bass pressure
Slow, irreversible harmonic movement
No melody, only gravity
(Music starts before first clear image)

🌑 VISUAL LANGUAGE (LOCKED)
Earth visible but emotionally irrelevant
Human structures look temporary, modular, unfinished
Alien organisms look ancient, rooted, geological
Biology partially fused with lunar regolith
No glowing sci-fi tropes — everything is matte, wet, mineral

🧬 BIOLOGICAL PHYSICS (CRITICAL)
Organisms are not aggressive
They respond slowly, with delayed causality
Internal motion visible:
fluid circulation
membrane contraction
eye-like nodules adjusting opacity
Growth happens between cuts, never on-screen
Scale subtly shifts — never emphasized

🎥 CAMERA BEHAVIOR
Handheld micro-jitter (helmet cam realism)
Slow lateral drift, never locked
Focus breathes imperfectly
Foreground steals focus unpredictably
Camera always feels slightly late, like it’s missing something

⏱️ SCENE FLOW (MONTAGE, NO HOLDS)

0.0–2.0s
Hard cut in.
Astronaut boots pass frame.
Behind them: blue translucent structures already present.
No one reacts.

2.0–4.0s
Macro snap — internal anatomy of organism.
Something solid embedded inside… metal.

4.0–6.0s
Wide but moving shot:
Antenna tower dwarfed by biological columns.
The organism is older than the base.

6.0–8.0s
Close-up:
Inside translucent tissue — a rusted human tool, half fossilized.

8.0–10.0s
Astronaut helmet reflection:
Structures appear closer than before.
No movement shown.

10.0–12.5s
Ground-level tracking shot.
Regolith transitions seamlessly into organic texture.

12.5–15.0s
Flash cut — underground cross-section:
Dead versions of the same organisms, layered deep.

15.0–17.0s
Radio crackle (no dialogue).
Subtle rhythmic pulse in organisms syncs to suit systems.

17.0–19.0s
Human base wall —
Biological growth has grown around it, not through it.

19.0–21.5s
Astronaut turns head.
Cut before we see what they see.

21.5–24.5s
Slow forward drift toward organisms.
They don’t react.
They don’t need to.

24.5–25.0s — END CARD (SEPARATE SCENE)
Fade to black.
Cut to silence.
Ends cleanly.

🔊 SFX PALETTE (SUBTLE)
Suit servo whine
Fine dust displacement
Wet internal biological movement
Low seismic creaks (planet-scale, not monster)

No roars.
No jump scares.
No impacts.

🧠 THEMATIC LOCK

Humans arrived late.
The moon adapted long ago.
Colonization was just another layer.